The minification itself was not really the issue. There really was a syntax error before minification. With `build.minify: false` in the `vite.config.js`, I could more easily figure out that a CSS rule got mutilated:

```css
@media print {
  /* … */
  .d-print-none {
    display: none !important;
  }
}
/* -- bootstrap ends here -- */

/* -- this is the point in time where the _transport.module.scss gets imported -- */

/* -- now vite continues without the rule header for the first component with styles -- */
  display: block;
  position: relative;
  min-height: var(--min-height);
}
.rv-spinner.stretch .spinner-border { /* … */ }
```

---

The **solution** is that the file with the export mustn't be "empty", aside from the export. It must contain at least one rule.

```scss
.irrelephant-rule-to-prevent-compilation-syntax-error { visibility: hidden; }

:export {
// …
```

Resulting in:

```css
._irrelephant-rule-to-prevent-compilation-syntax-error_v8f1_145 {
  visibility: hidden;
}.rv-spinner.stretch {
  --min-height: 0;
  display: block;
  position: relative;
  min-height: var(--min-height);
}
```

I'm surprised that after three years, this is still an issue.

I'm running into this problem when trying to split css (compiled from .scss) into style sheets and keep vendor css (bootstrap) separated. My style sheets need to be loaded last otherwise it messes up css cascading.

At least with Vue, the issue happens not because of the import order per se. Vite will keep the order of the imported style sheets but it will process the styles from Vue's SFC files first and the chunk that ends up including those styles will be added first to the style sheet import order. 

Consequently, if the main style (imported from the index js/ts) ends up in the same chunk as the SFC styles, it will be imported first along with them (because that style sheet is already on the top), regardless of its actual import order.

Haven't found a clean solution, I used a hack in the form of ad hoc plugin in my Vite config to transform the result html, remove the style sheet in question and append it to the end of `<head>`:
```typescript
{
  name: "html-transform",
  enforce: "post",
  transformIndexHtml(html) {
    // Match the entire line in html that includes that stylesheet that needs
    // to go last. Regex group to extract link href.
    const rxStyle = /^\s*<link rel="stylesheet".+href="(\/css\/MAKELAST\.css).*$\n/m;
    const tags: HtmlTagDescriptor[] = [];
    const m = html.match(rxStyle);
    if (m) {
      html = html.replace(rxStyle, "");
      tags.push({
        injectTo: "head",
        tag: "link",
        attrs: { rel: "stylesheet", crossorigin: true, href: m[1] }
      });
    }
    return { html, tags };
  }
}
```

This works well but it's another thing to track and remember to update if project structure changes.

5 years and it's still a thing.

But I found a cleaner solution instead of the HTML transformation hack:

Put all vendor style imports in a separate (css/scss) file and wrap it with css `@layer {}` (widely supported these days). Then import this file directly.

Vite chunk order and css loading order won't matter anymore because everything inside `@layer {}` will be always overriden by anything outside it.

For example, using bootstrap:

in _scss/bootstrap_layer.scss_:
```scss
@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/functions";
@import "./variables";

@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/variables";
@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/variables-dark";

@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/maps";
@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/mixins";
@import "../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/root";
@layer bootstrap {
    @import "@bootstrap/scss/root";
    @import "@bootstrap/scss/reboot";
    @import "@bootstrap/scss/containers";
    // ...
}
```

in _index.ts_:
```ts
import "./scss/bootstrap_layer.scss";
import "./scss/style.scss";
```

Then you can split/merge chunks/assets in vite config as needed, it will still override styles correctly.

