### 🔍 Additional Insight on Path Resolution

I’ve traced the path mismatch more precisely to this line in Vite’s `resolve.ts` plugin:

```ts
const basedir = importer ? path.dirname(importer) : process.cwd();
```
Ref: [https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/packages/vite/src/node/plugins/resolve.ts#L262](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/packages/vite/src/node/plugins/resolve.ts#L262)

This shows that when entering the build — for example, using `index.html` as input without explicitly setting `root` — Vite uses `process.cwd()` as the base directory. Later, however, `importer` is resolved using `fs.realpathSync.native()` (as part of Vite’s Windows-specific handling), which causes `basedir` to shift to the real volume (`H:/...`). This mismatch between the subst path and the real path leads to `path.relative()` returning an absolute path, which Rollup rejects.

To confirm this, I added a debug log to the compiled Vite source (`dist/node/chunks/dep-Bg4HVnP5.js`) just before line 11865 and ran `npm run build` inside of frontend folder.

```ts
console.log("### resolvePlugin::resolveId: ", fsPath, basedir, id);
```

> Watch how basedir mutates on windows with subst folder.

---

### ✅ TL;DR — Key Findings & Suggestions

- Vite uses `fs.realpathSync.native()` internally on Windows, which dereferences subst drives (e.g. `D:` → `H:\Workspace`)
- This causes `config.root` and resolved paths to mismatch, leading to Rollup errors like:
  ```
  [vite:build-html] The "fileName" or "name" properties of emitted chunks and assets must be strings that are neither absolute nor relative paths, received "H:/Workspace/..."
  ```
- The mismatch originates from `resolve.ts` using:
  ```ts
  const basedir = importer ? path.dirname(importer) : process.cwd();
  ```
- This means the initial resolution uses `process.cwd()` (subst path), but later `importer` is resolved to the real volume, causing inconsistency
- Suggested fix: default to `root: fs.realpathSync.native(path.resolve('./'))` to align paths from the start
- Alternatively: document this clearly in the Getting Started guide as a crucial Windows-specific note
- Long-term: centralize path resolution logic across plugins using separate plugin e.g. `vite/pathtamer`, inspired by [`upath`](https://github.com/anodynos/upath), to avoid inconsistent path handling.

---

