This issue is quite difficult to solve because `import './foo.css'` means "injecting `foo.css` and all other css into a single element".

I assume the OP wants to inject all styles that are transitively imported via `App.vue` — for example, styles imported by a module that is imported by `App.vue`.
Adding an option to control where CSS is injected (https://github.com/vitejs/vite/pull/12206) solves the use case where there's only a single custom element. However, it only works in that specific scenario.

Consider the following example with two custom elements (https://github.com/vitejs/vite/pull/12206#discussion_r1172211843):
```js
import AppA from './AppA.vue' // imports foo.css and a.css
const shadowHost = document.querySelector('#my-host-a');
const shadowRoot = shadowHost.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
createApp(AppA).mount(shadowRoot);

import AppB from './AppB.vue' // imports foo.css and b.css
const shadowHost = document.querySelector('#my-host-b');
const shadowRoot = shadowHost.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
createApp(AppB).mount(shadowRoot);
```
- `foo.css` and `a.css` should be injected into AppA's shadow root
- `foo.css` and `b.css` should be injected into AppB's shadow root

To achieve this behavior using that proposed option, users would need to manually determine which CSS should be injected into which elements, which I don't think is realistic:

```js
function whichElement(cssFileName) {
  switch (cssFileName) {
    case 'foo.css': return ['#my-host-a', '#my-host-b'];
    case 'a.css': return ['#my-host-a'];
    case 'b.css': return ['#my-host-b'];
  }
}
```
This approach also fails when elements are injected dynamically (https://github.com/vitejs/vite/pull/12206#issuecomment-1514417136). Since the injection target is determined at build time, there's no way to control *when* the CSS should be injected at runtime.

Vite does offer a way to opt out of automatic CSS injection using the [`.css?inline` import](https://vite.dev/guide/features.html#disabling-css-injection-into-the-page), which returns the CSS content. But this only works when you explicitly control the CSS imports.

To elaborate on my last comment, source map collapse (e.g. [rollup code](https://github.com/rollup/rollup/blob/19312a762c3cda56a0f6dc80a0887a4499db2257/src/utils/collapseSourcemaps.ts#L235)) traces mapping from the last one back to original source, so for the following plugin transform chain example, rollup's source map collapse should become trivial. But when any other plugin generates `map` in their `transform`, the cost for that cannot be avoided even though such `map` won't be used. (However, normally plugins tends to skip transform for `node_modules` by default, this might not be a practical concern.)

<details><summary>plugin transform chain</summary>

```js
{
  plugins: [
    {
      name: "someone's-plugin1",
      transform(code) {
        const result = babel.transform(code, {
          sourceMaps: true, // <-- unavoidable
          plugins: [/* ... some transform ... */]
        })
        return {
          code: result.code,
          map: result.map,
        }
      }
    },
    {
      name: "someone's-plugin2",
      transform(code) {
        const ms = new MagicString(code);
        // ... some transform ...
        return {
          code: ms.toString(),
          map: ms.generateMap({ hires: 'boundary' }) // <-- unavoidable
        }
      }
    },
    {
      name: "my-strip-mappings",
      transform(code) {
        return {
          code, 
          map: { mappings: '' }
        }
      }
    },
  ]
}
```

</details>

