So this issue is slightly more severe than I originally thought when I wrote the above description, because the workaround of using a custom plugin that i mentioned there, doesn't actually work.

If you want to disable writes while in watch mode, setting `build.write` to false is not enough. This is because in watch mode, [Rollup writes the files itself, unless the `skipWrite` watcher option is specified](https://github.com/rollup/rollup/blob/c7a890771f9b4b48748fa4789aee83f1921e32f1/src/watch/watch.ts#L212-L213). So you have do something like this in your Vite config:
```
{
	build: {
		// Don't write the output files to disk, we want to access them via the watcher API
		write: false,
		...
		// Enable watch mode
		watch: {
				// Prevent Rollup from writing the files to the disk.
				skipWrite: true,
		},
	},
	...
}
```
The problem now is that when you set `skipWrite` to true, this makes Rollup not call the output generation hooks. So you cannot rely on a plugin with a e.g. `generateBundle`/`writeBundle` hook like I originally thought.

Here's a [relevant comment](https://rollupjs.org/javascript-api/#rollup-watch) from Rollup's docs (emphasis mine):
> BUNDLE_END   — finished building a bundle
>                * event.input will be the input options object if present
>                * event.output contains an array of the "file" or
>                  "dir" option values of the generated outputs
>                * event.duration is the build duration in milliseconds
>                * event.result contains the bundle object that can be
>                  used to generate additional outputs by calling
>                  bundle.generate or bundle.write. **This is especially**
>                  **important when the watch.skipWrite option is used**.

@redneb In my experience, you can actually extract the bundle programmatically in watch mode without writing to disk and without using `build.write: false`. You manually clear out the bundle in the `generateBundle` hook. [Per the Rollup docs](https://rollupjs.org/plugin-development/#generatebundle:~:text=You%20can%20prevent%20files%20from%20being%20emitted%20by%20deleting%20them%20from%20the%20bundle%20object%20in%20this%20hook%2E):

> You can prevent files from being emitted by deleting them from the bundle object in this hook.

For example:

```ts
      generateBundle: {
        handler(_options, bundle) {
         const copy = { ...bundle };

         for (const fileName of Object.keys(bundle)) {
           delete bundle[fileName];
         }

          // Do something with the copied bundle.
        },
        order: "post",
      },
```

This approach has worked perfectly in a recent project of mine.

