@bluwy wanted to contribute another use case which may motivate exposing `req` and `res` as command-specific properties on `ctx` passed to `transformIndexHtml`... I'm working on a CSP plugin for Vite, which has the following minimal example of generating CSP nonces (https://stackblitz.com/edit/vitejs-vite-pckfyp?file=vite.config.js):

```js
let lastNonce;

const cspNonceDevPlugin = {
  name: 'csp-nonce',
  apply: 'serve',

  configureServer(server) {
    server.middlewares.use(
      util.callbackify(async (req, res) => {
        lastNonce = (await randomBytes(16)).toString('base64url');
        res.setHeader(
          'Content-Security-Policy',
          `script-src 'self' 'nonce-${lastNonce}'`
        );
      })
    );
  },

  transformIndexHtml(html) {
    const jsdom = new JSDOM(html);
    for (const script of jsdom.window.document.querySelectorAll('script')) {
      script.nonce = lastNonce;
    }
    return jsdom.serialize();
  },
};
```

Two drawbacks with the above implementation:

- Since the middleware does not know whether this request is for an HTML file, it ends up applying `Content-Security-Policy` for _all_ requests, not just HTML ones, even though `Content-Security-Policy` doesn't have any effect on, for example, image responses.

    If there was a way to access `res` within `transformIndexHtml`, the middleware logic can be moved there.
    
    Since Vite already does the work to determine whether a request is for an HTML file,

    https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/0fed2104ad07f0de4dbf29ed8d6047b6918c7db3/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L379-L401
    
    reimplementing the same within middleware doesn't seem like a good solution.

- Since there is no way to ensure that middleware and `transformIndexHtml` correspond to the same request, passing data using a global variable (`lastNonce`) seems unsafe. What if async requests are interleaved?

    1. Request A (url: `/`) calls async middleware and sets `lastNonce`
    2. Request B (url: `/@vite/client`) calls async middleware and overwrites `lastNonce`
    3. Request A calls `transformIndexHtml`, which consumes `lastNonce` from (ii) instead of (i)

    Even though `req.originalUrl` in middleware corresponds to `ctx.originalUrl` in `transformIndexHtml`, not all requests are HTML requests, so storing data keyed by `req.originalUrl` which is never consumed by `transformIndexHtml` also seems unworkable.

Circling back to the original ask, since `transformIndexHtml` already exposes context-specific parameters

https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/0fed2104ad07f0de4dbf29ed8d6047b6918c7db3/packages/vite/src/node/plugins/html.ts#L926-L939

it doesn't seem like such a bad idea to also expose `req` and `res` in `vite dev` and `vite preview` modes. WDYT?

 

