> Why do you need this?
> 
> If you want to know the relationships between the JS files and the CSS files, using [the manifest](https://vite.dev/config/build-options.html#build-manifest) would be the way, as a single CSS file may be shared among multiple entrypoints.

The manifest isn't helpful in this regard. For starters, going through 1000s of lines of manifest is far more difficult than looking at the file name. Second, the manifest doesn't actually map back to the source file properly. Possibly for the same reason the names are misleading: the CSS splitting code erases (or more likely omits) the metadata that shows where the file came from. 

```
{
  "_shared-chunk.js": {
    "file": "shared-chunk.js",
    "name": "some-js",
    "css": [
      "assets/some-js.css"
    ]
  },
  "_some-js.css": {
    "file": "assets/some-js.css",
    "src": "_some-js.css"
  },
  "src/index-2.ts": {
    "file": "my-app-entry-2.js",
    "name": "entry-2",
    "src": "src/index-2.ts",
    "isEntry": true,
    "imports": [
      "_shared-chunk.js"
    ],
    "css": [
      "assets/entry-2.css"
    ]
  },
  "src/index.ts": {
    "file": "my-app-entry-1.js",
    "name": "entry-1",
    "src": "src/index.ts",
    "isEntry": true,
    "imports": [
      "_shared-chunk.js"
    ],
    "css": [
      "assets/entry-1.css"
    ]
  }
}
```

As for why I need to know, it's because in a project with 100's of entry points and over 1000 output files, having file names which not only don't point to the right file, but point to the *wrong* file make verification and troubleshooting far more difficult than they have to be.

As an example, I discovered this when trying to verify if our migration from Webpack to Astro (which uses Vite) was generating reasonably similar output. The fact that the build was emitting a bunch of js files named `theme-font.css.js` when no such file existed. I spend a lot of time and effort trying to figure out why CSS files were causing JS files to be emitted. Unfortunately the content wasn't helpful in this regard and because the way the CSS splitter works causes all metadata about the source files to be erased I couldn't even write a plugin which could identify where the files came from.

